  "practice_data": {
    "method_card": {
      "title": "Two-Way Tables, Tree Diagrams and Venn Diagrams",
      "content": "<p>Three visual tools help you organise probability problems with two or three events.</p><p>A <strong>two-way table</strong> shows frequencies for two categorical variables. To find a probability, divide the relevant cell count by the row total, column total, or grand total &mdash; read the question carefully to identify the correct denominator.</p><p>A <strong>tree diagram</strong> shows sequential events. Each branch carries a probability; multiply along a branch to find the probability of that path: $$P(A \\text{ and } B) = P(A) \\times P(B | A)$$</p><p>Add the probabilities of separate branches that give the same outcome. For <strong>independent events</strong>, \\(P(B|A) = P(B)\\), so the second-branch probabilities are identical regardless of what happened first. For <strong>sampling without replacement</strong>, the denominators on the second level <em>decrease by 1</em> &mdash; this is the most common error.</p><p>A <strong>Venn diagram</strong> places outcomes in overlapping circles. The region inside both circles is \\(A \\cap B\\). The region outside all circles is &ldquo;neither&rdquo;. Total all regions to find the grand total for the denominator.</p><p><strong>Higher tier only &mdash; conditional probability notation:</strong> $$P(A|B) = \\frac{P(A \\text{ and } B)}{P(B)}$$ This formula will be given in the question. \\(P(A|B)\\) means &ldquo;the probability of A given that B has already occurred.&rdquo;</p>",
      "steps": [
        "Identify how many events are involved and whether they are sequential (tree) or categorical (two-way/Venn)",
        "For tree diagrams: write probabilities on each branch, then multiply along each complete path",
        "Adjust second-branch denominators if the problem says 'without replacement'",
        "Add path probabilities if the question asks for outcomes that can happen in more than one way",
        "For Venn diagrams: fill in the intersection first, then work outwards to each circle, then outside",
        "For conditional probability (Higher): divide P(A and B) by P(B)"
      ]
    },
    "exam_context": {
      "paper": "Paper 1 and Paper 2",
      "marks": "3 to 6 marks; tree diagram questions often include a 2-mark structured calculation followed by a 2-mark without-replacement extension",
      "frequency": "Tree diagrams and two-way tables appear on nearly every paper; Venn diagrams appear regularly; conditional probability notation is tested at Higher tier"
    },
    "worked_examples": [
      {
        "difficulty": "Bronze",
        "question": "The two-way table below shows sports preferences for 50 students. A student is chosen at random. What is the probability the student prefers swimming?",
        "steps": [
          {
            "label": "Step 1 — Read the table",
            "content": "<table class=\"data-table\"><tr><th></th><th>Football</th><th>Swimming</th><th>Total</th></tr><tr><td>Boys</td><td>12</td><td>8</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>Girls</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>27</td><td>23</td><td>50</td></tr></table><p>Total students who prefer swimming = 8 + 15 = 23.</p>"
          },
          {
            "label": "Step 2 — Write the probability",
            "content": "<p>Total students = 50. \\(P(\\text{swimming}) = \\frac{23}{50}\\)</p>"
          },
          {
            "label": "Answer",
            "content": "<p><strong>\\(P(\\text{swimming}) = \\dfrac{23}{50}\\)</strong></p>",
            "isAnswer": true
          }
        ]
      },
